            <ArticleTitle>Postcoital vaginal cuff rupture 10 months after a total vaginal hysterectomy. A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>238-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Transvaginal evisceration following total vaginal hysterectomy secondary to coitus is extremely rare.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A woman presented 10 months following a total vaginal hysterectomy with complaints of progressive postcoital abdominal and shoulder pain as well as a pinkish vaginal discharge. Examination revealed a 3-cm defect at the left edge of the vaginal cuff. Corrective surgery followed overnight observation with pain management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Postcoital vaginal cuff disruption is rare, and complications can range from bowel evisceration to hemorrhage. Management should be tailored to the severity of the complications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
